1
00:00:00,112 --> 00:00:01,250
We wake up every day,

2
00:00:01,512 --> 00:00:02,100
go to work,

3
00:00:02,488 --> 00:00:05,325
and feel that this routine is absolutely real.

4
00:00:05,750 --> 00:00:07,888
But in his conversation with Lex Fridman,

5
00:00:08,238 --> 00:00:11,025
Joscha Bach describes our existence in a way

6
00:00:11,038 --> 00:00:12,362
that is hard to unsee.

7
00:00:12,788 --> 00:00:14,900
His claim is simple and unsettling:

8
00:00:15,362 --> 00:00:17,263
you have never actually lived in the physical

9
00:00:17,275 --> 00:00:17,600
world.

10
00:00:18,050 --> 00:00:19,312
Think about where your brain is.

11
00:00:19,725 --> 00:00:21,213
It sits in total darkness,

12
00:00:21,450 --> 00:00:22,900
sealed inside your skull.

13
00:00:23,325 --> 00:00:24,200
It cannot see,

14
00:00:24,600 --> 00:00:26,688
hear, or touch anything directly.

15
00:00:27,112 --> 00:00:29,462
All it ever receives are electrical signals,

16
00:00:29,725 --> 00:00:31,575
spikes travelling along nerves.

17
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:33,250
So where does the world come from?

18
00:00:33,675 --> 00:00:35,238
The colors, the sounds,

19
00:00:35,475 --> 00:00:37,225
the geometry of the room around you?

20
00:00:37,650 --> 00:00:39,462
Bach's answer is that your brain builds it.

21
00:00:39,875 --> 00:00:41,888
Everything you see is a kind of virtual

22
00:00:41,900 --> 00:00:42,500
reality,

23
00:00:42,875 --> 00:00:45,325
a model generated to explain those signals.

24
00:00:45,750 --> 00:00:48,125
Your brain runs something like the physics engine

25
00:00:48,138 --> 00:00:49,075
of a video game,

26
00:00:49,488 --> 00:00:52,350
a fast, simplified simulation of objects,

27
00:00:52,525 --> 00:00:54,900
gravity, faces and space.

28
00:00:55,312 --> 00:00:56,800
It is not a perfect copy of the

29
00:00:56,812 --> 00:00:57,638
outside world.

30
00:00:58,050 --> 00:00:59,562
It is a useful story that lets a

31
00:00:59,575 --> 00:01:00,662
primate survive.

32
00:01:01,075 --> 00:01:02,500
And the physical world itself?

33
00:01:02,950 --> 00:01:05,350
Bach suggests it is something very different from

34
00:01:05,362 --> 00:01:06,275
what we perceive:

35
00:01:06,662 --> 00:01:09,337
a vast pattern of quantum mechanical processes,

36
00:01:09,600 --> 00:01:12,212
too fine and too fast to ever experience

37
00:01:12,225 --> 00:01:12,738
directly.

38
00:01:13,150 --> 00:01:15,200
What we call reality is the dream that

39
00:01:15,212 --> 00:01:16,962
our nervous system tells about it.

40
00:01:17,375 --> 00:01:18,350
You are not in the world.

41
00:01:18,762 --> 00:01:20,762
You are in a multimedia novel that your

42
00:01:20,775 --> 00:01:22,012
brain keeps writing,

43
00:01:22,325 --> 00:01:23,238
moment by moment.

44
00:01:23,650 --> 00:01:25,450
So why would our minds build such an

45
00:01:25,462 --> 00:01:26,388
elaborate illusion?

46
00:01:26,800 --> 00:01:28,675
And what does that illusion hide about where

47
00:01:28,688 --> 00:01:29,837
humanity is heading?

48
00:01:30,250 --> 00:01:31,700
We will answer both by the end of

49
00:01:31,712 --> 00:01:32,275
this video.

50
00:01:32,700 --> 00:01:33,262
But first,

51
00:01:33,662 --> 00:01:35,050
let's look at what our stories have done

52
00:01:35,062 --> 00:01:35,700
to the planet.
